  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 901 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n8PM radar imagery depicts a complex of storms diving south out of\nKentucky into Middle Tennessee. The 00Z sounding out of Nashville\ndepicts surface cape greater than 4500 J/kg along with bulk shear\nnearing 30 kts. While shear is on the weaker side, the abundance\nof instability will be enough for storms to overcome the weaker\nkinematics. One thing to note on the sounding is a weak EML at\naround 750 mb. The CAMs are modeling this well, depicting\nincreasing CIN over the next several hours. Given this, storms\nwill struggle to maintain strength for long and likely diminish\nby midnight. A Severe Thunderstorm Watch is in effect until 1AM\nfor areas along the Tennessee River. Damaging winds and heavy\nrainfall are the primary concerns at this time.\n\nHot and humid conditions resume on Monday as the eastern flank of\na 598 dam ridge remains over the Mid-South. Afternoon highs will\nreach the upper 90s areawide with heat indices in excess of\n110 degrees. This pattern will persist into Tuesday as\ntemperatures approach 100 degrees. An Extreme Heat Warning and\nHeat Advisory are in effect through 8PM Tuesday. Luckily, a\nbackdoor cold front will swing through the Mid-South on Wednesday\nand decrease dewpoints into the 60s. This will produce heat\nindices beneath headline criteria by Thursday. Until then, make\nsure you monitor yourself for signs of heat-related illness if\nworking outdoors. Take frequent breaks and remember to drink\nplenty of water.\n\nBy Friday, a shortwave trough will move through the central\nPlains. This feature will increase rain chances in the Mid-South\nthrough the weekend. It remains too early to tell specifics, but\nfor now expect the upcoming weekend to be wet and unsettled.\n\nANS",
